CAT = "sgsnbLegal"; SEG = "LEG"
Q = [
("A patient's relative phones the ward and asks for the patient's diagnosis. What does the SNB Code expect the nurse to do?",
 'Share only what is relevant and permitted', ['Give it if the caller sounds very worried', 'Give it if the caller says she is a close relative', 'Give it and note the call in the chart later'],
 "The Code says nurses safeguard confidentiality and only relevant information is shared with others directly involved in, and necessary for, the person's care. Worry or kinship alone is not a justification."),
("When may a nurse disclose confidential information without the person's consent, under the Code?",
 'When the law requires it', ['When a colleague asks out of interest', 'When the employer asks for statistics', 'When the family is upset'],
 'The Code accepts disclosure without consent only with sound justification, such as disclosure mandated by law or necessary to protect the person or others from a serious risk of harm.'),
('Mr Lim has died. A reporter asks the nurse about his illness. What applies?',
 'Confidentiality continues after death', ['Confidentiality ends once the patient dies', 'Only the ward manager is bound afterwards', 'Only written records stay protected'],
 "The Code states that a person's right to privacy and confidentiality continues after death, so the nurse does not discuss his illness with the reporter."),
('A nurse sees a colleague repeatedly using an unsafe technique that could harm patients. What does the Code expect?',
 'Report the unsafe practice', ['Warn the colleague and say nothing more', 'Wait to see whether harm occurs', 'Discuss it with other staff informally'],
 'Advocating for people includes reporting unsafe clinical practices that could compromise safety, through the proper channel and the appropriate authority.'),
('A nurse is asked to carry out a procedure she has never been trained to do. Which response fits the Code?',
 'Decline and escalate the concern', ['Do it carefully using the manual', 'Do it if the doctor is nearby', 'Do it because the ward is short-staffed'],
 'Nurses must recognise and work within the limits of their competence and scope of practice, and escalate concerns when asked to practise beyond their scope, experience and training.'),
('By when should a nurse complete documentation of care under the Code?',
 'At the time, or as soon as possible', ['By the end of the working week', 'Only when the care given is unusual', 'After a supervisor has reviewed the plan'],
 'Principle 7 says records should be completed at the time or as soon as possible after an event, so that they are accurate and timely.'),
("Which entry in the chart meets the Code's expectations for records?",
 'Clear, dated, timed and signed', ['Brief, with common slang shortened', 'Typed later from memory by a friend', 'Initialled once for the whole shift'],
 'Entries must be clear, dated, timed and signed or initialled, free of unauthorised abbreviations, jargon and speculation, and kept secure.'),
('Which of these must a nurse hold, besides registration, to practise lawfully under the Code?',
 'A valid Practising Certificate', ['A hospital staff pass', 'A postgraduate qualification', 'A professional indemnity letter'],
 'Under Principle 9 on self-regulation, nurses maintain a valid Practising Certificate and comply with all regulatory policies and guidelines.'),
("A nurse is accountable for her decisions and actions to which three parties, according to the Code's glossary?",
 'People, her employer and the SNB', ['Patients, doctors and the media', 'Her family, peers and the union', 'The ward, the pharmacy and the court'],
 "The glossary defines accountability as being answerable to people in care, the employer and the Singapore Nursing Board for one's professional judgments, decisions and actions."),
("A grateful patient's family offers the nurse an expensive watch. What does the Code say about gifts?",
 'Follow institutional policy on gifts', ['Accept it so as not to offend', 'Accept it if it is given publicly', 'Accept it and share it with the team'],
 'Nurses must adhere to institutional policies on accepting gifts, money, hospitality or inducements so they do not become beholden to any person or organisation.'),
('Which behaviour is a boundary violation rather than a brief boundary crossing?',
 'Keeping secrets with a patient', ['Staying a few minutes past shift for a patient', 'Offering a sweet to a distressed child', "Attending a patient's funeral service"],
 "Violations come from confusion between the nurse's needs and the patient's and are marked by secrecy, excessive personal disclosure or role reversal; keeping secrets with a patient is a listed red flag."),
("Which is a red flag behaviour listed in the Code's guidance on professional boundaries?",
 'Believing only you can help the client', ['Documenting time spent with a client', 'Asking a colleague for advice on boundaries', "Declining a patient's invitation to dinner"],
 'Thinking you are the only one who truly understands or can help a client is a warning sign that should prompt the nurse to examine the relationship.'),
('A current patient sends a nurse a friend request on her personal social media account. What is best?',
 'Decline and keep the boundary', ['Accept but post nothing about care', 'Accept if the patient is elderly', 'Accept after the patient is discharged'],
 'The guidance says not to accept or initiate client friend requests on personal accounts; any professional contact should be through a separate professional account.'),
('A nurse posts a ward photo with no names or faces visible. Why can this still breach confidentiality?',
 'Leaving out details does not protect identity', ['Photos are always banned on every ward', 'Nurses may not own a camera phone', 'Only posts with text can be traced back'],
 'The social media guidance warns that leaving out details when posting information or images does not protect client confidentiality; bed numbers or colleagues in view can identify people.'),
('A nurse learns a colleague has posted patient information online. What should she do?',
 'Report the breach to the right person at once', ['Message the colleague asking them to delete it', 'Ignore it if the post was only private', 'Wait to see whether anyone complains first'],
 'The guidance says confidentiality breaches must be reported immediately to the right person, because content spreads quickly even if later removed.'),
('What can happen if a nurse uses personal social media while on duty?',
 'It could be viewed as client abandonment', ['It is allowed during any short break', 'It counts towards continuing education', 'It is a minor boundary crossing only'],
 "The Code's guidance says personal use of social media while working could be viewed as client abandonment, because the nurse's obligations to clients come first."),
("An adult with capacity refuses a recommended treatment after being informed. What does the Code's autonomy principle say?",
 'Respect the right to refuse treatment', ['Persuade him until the person agrees', 'Treat anyway to avoid harm to him', 'Ask the family to override his choice'],
 'People have the right to voluntarily accept, refuse or terminate treatment, provided they have been given accurate information in a form that supports an informed decision.'),
('A patient lacks capacity to decide on a procedure. From whom should the nurse seek consent?',
 'A legally authorised representative', ['Any friend who happens to be visiting', 'The nurse in charge of the shift alone', 'A colleague of the patient from work'],
 "Where a person cannot decide, the Code expects the nurse to seek the consent of a legally authorised representative while keeping the person's rights and best interests at the centre."),
("Which action best shows respect for an elderly patient's cultural needs?",
 'Ask about preferences and adapt care', ['Apply the ward routine to everyone alike', "Assume needs from the patient's ethnicity", 'Leave cultural needs to the family only'],
 "The Code requires care determined by people's values, needs and rights, with cultural sensitivities considered, without assuming needs from a group profile."),
("A nurse's own religious views conflict with a patient's request for a plain, non-religious bedside service. What does the Code expect?",
 'Provide care without prejudice', ['Refuse to nurse the patient', 'Suggest the patient change the request', 'Offer care only to some patients'],
 'Care must be provided without unfair or prejudicial discrimination on the basis of religion or any other attribute, and the safety and wellbeing of the person come first.'),
("Which situation fits the Code's definition of professional misconduct?",
 'Abusing the client relationship', ['Asking for a second opinion on care', 'Declining an unsafe assignment', 'Taking approved annual leave'],
 'The glossary defines misconduct as an act or omission that deviates from the Code, abuses the professional relationship with the client or brings the profession into disrepute.'),
("A nurse intends to publish an article about her ward's practice. Which duty comes with this?",
 'Ensure the content is accurate and ethical', ['Publish quickly before the practice changes', 'Keep her credentials out of the paper', 'Include patient photos for impact'],
 "The Code says published material must be accurate, responsible and ethical, reflect the nurse's relevant skills, experience and qualifications, and follow institutional and ethical research requirements."),
('Under the Code, what is a nurse responsible for regarding her own wellbeing?',
 'Maintaining her fitness to practise', ['Nothing, as the employer decides', 'Reporting only physical illness', 'Taking leave whenever she feels stressed'],
 "Self-regulation includes responsibility for one's own health, safety and wellbeing, with physical, psychological and emotional fitness to practise."),
('A nurse is asked to collect data from patients for a ward research project. What does the Code expect first?',
 'Approval from the proper review board', ['Verbal agreement from the ward manager', "Consent from the nurse's colleagues", "Payment for the patients' time"],
 'Research must follow ethical principles and institutional guidelines and, where appropriate, be approved by the institutional review board or regulatory authority.'),
]
